; StyleBoxFlat's raster, on four Panels of identical size over a light backdrop.
;
; The one variable: the shape a StyleBoxFlat draws. Same node type, same
; 480x260 rect, same grid pitch, same fill colour on the three that draw one —
; only the box's own geometry fields change, so a wrong ring or a wrong corner
; can only be the geometry builder.
;
; Uniform is the control: one radius on all four corners, no border, so it
; reads as a plain rounded rectangle and pins the fill arc alone.
;
; Asymmetric and Blended are a PAIR that differs in exactly one field,
; `border_blend`. Both carry per-corner radii (0 / 64 / 0 / 64, so a builder
; that applied one radius everywhere is obvious) and a uniform 16 px border in
; a colour far from the fill. With the blend off, the border is a flat ring and
; the fill starts at a hard edge; with it on, Godot interpolates the ring's
; inner vertices toward the fill and the same 16 px reads as a gradient. That
; second image is the ONLY place the blend appears anywhere in the goldens: it
; changes no silhouette at all, so a raster that ignored the flag would leave
; every outline in every other scene exactly where it is.
;
; Edges pins the two remaining fields together — four DIFFERENT border widths
; (6 / 20 / 40 / 12) and `draw_center = false`. The widths are all unequal so a
; ring built from one side's width mis-sizes three edges, and with no centre
; drawn the pale backdrop shows through the middle, which is what proves the
; ring is a ring rather than a filled box wearing a border colour.
;
; The backdrop is deliberately much lighter than every box: the corner arcs and
; the hollow centre are only measurable against something they contrast with,
; and the goldens' other 2D scenes all sit on dark ground.
;
; Measured against Godot 4.6.3 at `--mode 2d`: Uniform, Asymmetric and Edges
; match to within corner-arc antialiasing (at most 0.3% of a pane's area, and
; 95-100% of that within one radius of a corner), and the ring itself is
; pixel-exact wherever it is not blended — a transect across
; Asymmetric's left edge at y=178 reads rgb(242, 191, 51) for all 16 border
; pixels then rgb(38, 76, 128) on both sides. Blended is where the two part
; company: Godot ramps the ring toward the fill in sRGB, so its midpoint at
; x=616, y=470 is rgb(137, 132, 90), while ours interpolates the same two
; endpoints through the linear pipeline and reads rgb(175, 145, 101) there. Both
; ends of the ramp agree; only its shape differs. The baseline below is OURS, so
; that gap lives inside Blended — 15% of that pane — and nowhere else in the frame.

[gd_scene load_steps=5 format=3]

[sub_resource type="StyleBoxFlat" id="StyleBoxFlat_uniform"]
bg_color = Color(0.15, 0.3, 0.5, 1)
corner_radius_top_left = 40
corner_radius_top_right = 40
corner_radius_bottom_right = 40
corner_radius_bottom_left = 40

[sub_resource type="StyleBoxFlat" id="StyleBoxFlat_asymmetric"]
bg_color = Color(0.15, 0.3, 0.5, 1)
border_width_left = 16
border_width_top = 16
border_width_right = 16
border_width_bottom = 16
border_color = Color(0.95, 0.75, 0.2, 1)
corner_radius_top_left = 0
corner_radius_top_right = 64
corner_radius_bottom_right = 0
corner_radius_bottom_left = 64

[sub_resource type="StyleBoxFlat" id="StyleBoxFlat_blended"]
bg_color = Color(0.15, 0.3, 0.5, 1)
border_width_left = 16
border_width_top = 16
border_width_right = 16
border_width_bottom = 16
border_color = Color(0.95, 0.75, 0.2, 1)
border_blend = true
corner_radius_top_left = 0
corner_radius_top_right = 64
corner_radius_bottom_right = 0
corner_radius_bottom_left = 64

[sub_resource type="StyleBoxFlat" id="StyleBoxFlat_edges"]
